.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 17, 2026
.Dt MOUNT 8
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm mount ,
.Nm mount_nullfs ,
.Nm mount_tmpfs
.Nd mount file systems
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Nm
.Fl a
.Op Fl v
.Op Fl t Ar type Ns Op , Ns Ar type ...
.Nm
.Op Fl v
.Op Fl t Ar type
.Op Fl o Ar options
.Ar special node
.Nm
.Op Fl v
.Ar special | node
.Nm
.Fl -bind
.Ar directory node
.Nm mount_nullfs
.Op Fl o Ar options
.Ar target node
.Nm mount_tmpfs
.Op Fl o Ar options
.Cm tmpfs Ar node
.Nm
.Fl u
.Op Fl rvw
.Op Fl o Ar options
.Ar node | special
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
attaches a file system to the directory
.Ar node ,
which then shows the file system's contents instead of its own, with
.Xr nmount 2 .
With no arguments it lists what is mounted.
.Pp
The file system types are:
.Bl -tag -width nullfs
.It Cm tmpfs
A new, empty file system in memory; its
.Ar special
is conventionally
.Dq tmpfs
.Pq Xr tmpfs 5 .
.It Cm nullfs
The directory
.Ar special
(the
.Ar target ) ,
of any file system, seen again at
.Ar node ;
what is created or removed in one is in the other
.Pq Xr nullfs 5 .
Linux calls this a bind mount, hence
.Fl -bind .
.It Cm devfs
A view of the devices
.Pq Xr devfs 5 ;
the kernel mounts one on
.Pa /dev .
.El
.Pp
With a single
.Ar special
or
.Ar node ,
the rest is taken from its line in
.Xr fstab 5 .
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl a
Mount every file system in
.Xr fstab 5
that isn't mounted, isn't marked
.Cm noauto ,
and isn't the root, in the order listed.
.It Fl o Ar options
Comma-separated mount options:
.Bl -tag -width wxallowed
.It Cm rw
Read-write, the default.
.It Cm ro
Read-only: nothing on it can be created, changed or removed
.Pq Er EROFS .
.It Cm noexec
Run no program from the file system
.Pq Er EACCES .
.It Cm nosuid
Ignore the set-user-ID and set-group-ID bits of programs on the file
system.
.It Cm wxallowed
Let programs on the file system that are marked as needing it (linked with
.Fl z Cm wxneeded )
map memory writable and executable at once, which is otherwise refused.
Mount only a tree no one but the superuser can write.
.El
.Pp
Any other option is the type's, which refuses one it doesn't take:
.Cm size ,
.Cm nodes ,
.Cm maxfilesize ,
.Cm mode ,
.Cm uid
and
.Cm gid
for
.Cm tmpfs .
.It Fl r
With
.Fl u :
read-only, the same as
.Fl o Cm ro .
.It Fl t Ar type
The type to mount; with
.Fl a ,
the types to mount, or with a leading
.Dq no ,
the types not to.
.It Fl u
Change a mounted file system: between read-only and read-write
.Pq Fl r , Fl w ; No without either it stays as it is ,
and its type's
.Ar options
.Pq Cm size No for Cm tmpfs .
The kernel mounts the root read-only at boot, and
.Xr rc 8
mounts it read-write once it's been checked.
Read-write to read-only fails while a file on it is open for writing.
.It Fl v
Name each file system mounted.
.It Fl w
With
.Fl u :
read-write, the same as
.Fl o Cm rw .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /proc/mounts -compact
.It Pa /etc/fstab
the file systems to mount
.It Pa /proc/mounts
what is mounted
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mount -t tmpfs -o size=64m tmpfs /mnt/scratch
mount -u -o size=128m /mnt/scratch
mount -t nullfs -o ro /usr/src /mnt/src
mount_nullfs /usr/src /mnt/src
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr nmount 2 ,
.Xr devfs 5 ,
.Xr fstab 5 ,
.Xr nullfs 5 ,
.Xr tmpfs 5 ,
.Xr umount 8
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
command appeared in
.At v1 .
